    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The Texas seller disclosure notice is a written form that most home sellers must give buyers, describing what the seller knows about the property's condition. It is required by Texas Property Code Section 5.008 for resales of single-family homes, and it must be delivered on or before the contract's effective date. Here is what the form covers, how the flood questions work, who is exempt and what happens if a seller gets it wrong.</p>

<h2>What the Texas seller disclosure notice is (and is not)</h2>
<p>Section 5.008 requires a seller of residential real property "comprising not more than one dwelling unit" in Texas to give the buyer a written notice. The statute spells out the minimum content. Sellers may use the statutory notice or one that is substantially similar, as long as it includes at least every item the law lists.</p>
<p>The notice itself states that it is a disclosure of the seller's knowledge as of the date signed. It is not a warranty and not a substitute for inspections. That framing matters for both sides: the buyer should still inspect, and the seller is judged on what they actually knew.</p>
<p>The Texas Real Estate Commission publishes its own Seller's Disclosure Notice, which tracks the statute and adds some questions. TREC adopted a revised version in 2026 that, according to TREC and industry summaries, adds questions on items such as current insurance coverage and conservation easements. You can find the current version on <a href="https://www.trec.texas.gov/node/3074">TREC's Seller's Disclosure Notice page</a>. Ask your agent which version you are signing, since Realtor association forms are also widely used in Houston.</p>

<h2>Who must give the notice, and when</h2>
<p>If you are selling a previously occupied single-family home in Texas, assume you owe the notice unless one of the exemptions below clearly applies. That includes homes sold "as-is." Our guide to <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-as-is-texas/">selling a house as-is in Texas</a> explains why an as-is sale does not remove the disclosure duty.</p>
<p>Timing is set by the statute. The notice must be delivered on or before the effective date of a contract binding the buyer to purchase. If a contract is signed without the notice, the buyer may terminate for any reason within seven days after receiving it. In practice, a late disclosure hands the buyer a free walk-away right, so most agents have sellers complete it before the house goes on the market.</p>

<h2>What must be disclosed on the form</h2>
<p>The statutory notice is organized into numbered sections. You answer Yes, No or Unknown based on what you know, and explain any "yes" answers. The main areas are:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Items on the property</strong> and whether any are not working or need repair, from appliances and HVAC to the roof type and age. Since 2023 the statutory list includes the type of fuel gas piping, such as black iron pipe or corrugated stainless steel tubing.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke detectors</strong>, and whether they meet Chapter 766 of the Health and Safety Code.</li>
<li><strong>Known defects</strong> in walls, ceilings, floors, roof, foundation, plumbing, electrical and other structural components.</li>
<li><strong>Known conditions</strong> such as active termites, previous structural or roof repair, water damage not due to a flood event, previous fires, lead-based paint, asbestos, radon, improper drainage, unplatted easements and previous use for methamphetamine manufacture.</li>
<li><strong>Any item or system in need of repair.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Flood conditions and flood zone location</strong>, covered in the next section.</li>
<li><strong>Prior flood insurance claims</strong>, including claims with the National Flood Insurance Program.</li>
<li><strong>FEMA or SBA assistance</strong> received for flood damage to the property.</li>
<li><strong>Other matters</strong>, including unpermitted additions or repairs, HOA fees or assessments, notices of deed restriction or code violations, lawsuits affecting the property, conditions affecting health or safety, and location in a groundwater conservation or subsidence district.</li>
</ol>
<p>The notice also includes standard statements about coastal properties near the Gulf and properties near military installations.</p>
<h3>What you do not have to disclose</h3>
<p>Section 5.008 says a seller or agent has no duty to disclose a death on the property by natural causes, suicide or an accident unrelated to the property's condition. It also says there is no duty to disclose whether a previous occupant had HIV or AIDS.</p>

<h2>The flood questions every Houston seller should understand</h2>
<p>After Hurricane Harvey, the Legislature expanded the flood portion of the notice through House Bill 3815, effective September 1, 2019. You may see this described as a 2022 change. That date actually belongs to a separate law requiring landlords to give tenants a floodplain and flood notice for leases signed or renewed on or after January 1, 2022. For home sales, the flood questions have been on the notice since 2019.</p>
<p>The flood section asks whether you are aware of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present flood insurance coverage.</li>
<li>Previous flooding due to a failure, breach or controlled release of a reservoir.</li>
<li>Previous water penetration into a structure due to a natural flood event.</li>
<li>Whether the property is wholly or partly in a 100-year floodplain, 500-year floodplain, floodway, flood pool or reservoir.</li>
</ul>
<p>Separate questions ask whether you have ever filed a flood damage claim with any insurer, including the NFIP, and whether you have received FEMA or SBA assistance for flood damage.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Term on the notice</th><th>What it means under the statute</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>100-year floodplain</td><td>A FEMA special flood hazard area (Zone A, V, A99, AE, AO, AH, VE or AR) with a one percent annual chance of flooding</td></tr>
<tr><td>500-year floodplain</td><td>A moderate flood hazard area, shown as shaded Zone X, with a 0.2 percent annual chance of flooding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Floodway</td><td>The regulatory floodway on the flood map: the channel and nearby land reserved to carry a base flood</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood pool</td><td>Land next to a U.S. Army Corps of Engineers reservoir, above its normal maximum level, subject to controlled flooding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reservoir</td><td>A water impoundment project operated by the Corps of Engineers to hold water or delay runoff</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The flood zone answers come from the current FEMA flood insurance rate map, so check the map rather than guessing. The flood pool and reservoir questions matter in west Houston near the Addicks and Barker reservoirs. For the buyer's side of this, read our guide to <a href="/blog/buying-a-house-in-a-flood-zone-houston/">buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</a>.</p>

<h2>Exemptions: when the notice is not required</h2>
<p>Section 5.008(e) lists transfers that do not require the notice. The most common ones are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Transfers under a court order or foreclosure sale, and sales by a lender that acquired the property through foreclosure or a deed in lieu.</li>
<li>Transfers by a bankruptcy trustee.</li>
<li>Transfers by a fiduciary administering a decedent's estate, guardianship, conservatorship or trust.</li>
<li>Transfers from one co-owner to another.</li>
<li>Transfers to a spouse or to lineal relatives, such as children or parents.</li>
<li>Transfers between spouses under a divorce or legal separation decree.</li>
<li>Transfers to or from a government entity.</li>
<li>Sales of a new home that has never been occupied.</li>
<li>Property where the value of any dwelling is no more than five percent of the property's value, such as raw land with a small structure.</li>
</ul>
<p>Being exempt from the form does not exempt anyone from honesty. An executor or a bank can still face fraud claims for actively hiding a known defect, and buyers' agents in these sales often ask for whatever information is available.</p>

<h2>Consequences of not disclosing</h2>
<p>The immediate consequence of a late or missing notice is the buyer's seven-day termination right. The bigger risk shows up after closing. Texas buyers who discover a known defect that was concealed may bring claims for common-law fraud, statutory real estate fraud or violations of the Texas Deceptive Trade Practices Act, depending on the facts. An "as-is" clause generally will not protect a seller who fraudulently concealed a defect.</p>
<p>The law also protects honest sellers. The statute says the notice is completed to the best of your belief and knowledge, and if you do not know something, marking it unknown is compliance. Problems come from answering "no" to something you knew, or "unknown" to something you clearly should have disclosed.</p>
<p>If you are unsure how to answer a question about a past claim, repair or legal dispute, confirm with a Texas real estate attorney before you sign.</p>

<h2>How to fill out the notice the right way</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gather records first:</strong> repair invoices, insurance claims, permits, foundation and roof warranties, HOA documents and any prior inspection reports.</li>
<li><strong>Answer every line.</strong> Blank lines invite questions and can look like evasion.</li>
<li><strong>Explain "yes" answers specifically:</strong> what happened, when, what was repaired, by whom, and whether a warranty transfers.</li>
<li><strong>Update it</strong> if you learn something new before closing, such as storm damage while the house is listed.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a signed copy</strong> with the buyer's acknowledgment, along with your supporting documents.</li>
</ul>
[[product:accordion-file-organizer]]
<p>Disclosed items affect price and negotiations, so build them into your expectations early. Our <a href="/tools/seller-net-proceeds-calculator/">seller net proceeds calculator</a> helps you see how repair credits or price reductions change what you walk away with. If a past fire is part of your history, see our guide on how to <a href="/blog/sell-fire-damaged-house-texas/">sell a fire damaged house in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The Texas seller disclosure notice is required for most single-family resales, it must be delivered by the contract's effective date, and it asks detailed questions about defects, repairs, flooding and legal issues. Fill it out early, honestly and completely, and back up your answers with records. Done well, it protects you far more than it costs you.</p>
